[Music]  if there's one thing that Star Wars  Battlefront 2 accomplishes well it's the  feeling of being in the exciting  universe of the legendary film series  serving up the greatest hits of all  things Star Wars the follow-up to dices  2015 multiplayer focused game presents a  greater package of content over its  predecessor but overall it's weighed  down by an overbearing and convoluted  progression system that doesn't seem to  value the average players time obscuring  an otherwise solid Star Wars experience  set across the backdrop of the entire  Star Wars saga Battlefront 2 expands the  scope of its galactic battles to great  effect from taking part in aerial  dogfights above Kamino to raiding the  Death Star - and escaping before its  destruction  the sequel puts its 14 Maps set across  the 40-year history of the series to  good use showing a clear difference in  aesthetics and tone from one time period  to the next  what's the plan come on keep getting  started for your snake the rebel can't  learn about on reason cinder  alike the first battlefront the sequel  contains a narrative driven  single-player campaign showing the fall  of the Empire and the eventual dawn of  the first order you take on the role of  iDEN versio commander of the Imperial  Special Forces outfit and Ferno squad  she normally works to undermine rebel  forces with covert missions and other  forms of espionage but after the fall of  the second Death Star her loyalties to  the Empire is tested when an  increasingly desperate Imperial Army  takes drastic measures to ensure its  future but they're not cool for you come  on diverse here is our target one of  them yes why the entire planet and its  people they're they're loyal to the  Empire the Emperor commands it will  purge this planet and others fear shall  spread and the galaxy will remember who  is in control commands while the brisk  four to five hour campaign features some  strong writing and performances from his  cast with some standout levels that show  off the visual lustre and diversity of  locations within the universe the  potential of its Imperial point-of-view  soon becomes lost falling into some  rather predictable twists the story  eventually turns into a familiar by the  number Star Wars adventure where the  good guys and bad guys are clearly  defined and with the lead up to the  final acts confrontation that signposted  from a mile away  if the Empire's here what I sensed was  true there must be something special  about this place  on occasion the campaign jumps to levels  that feature familiar faces adding some  moments of levity to the story those fun  and refreshing as they can be they come  off a bit too much like fanservice  leading Ida and versio to stand in the  shadow of others this is made worse by  an abrupt ending that teases future  updates to the campaign instead of  delivering a strong conclusion for its  hero's journey with that said the  campaign does a fairly decent job of  showing the internal strife within the  Empire's ranks even allowing you to  explore an imperial civilization on  ITINs homeworld of bardos but overall it  falls a bit short of making it a  remarkable journey for its characters  outside of the campaign and massive  multiplayer battles there are side modes  that offer some interesting diversions  the Arcade Mode makes a return featuring  themed levels where you battle AI BOTS  as classic light and dark side  characters  moreover the fan-favorite heroes vs.  villains mode is back letting players  cut loose with their favorite characters  cutting out unnecessary filler players  can choose their unlocked characters and  compete in four and four battles in over  the top and ridiculous fashion of your  time will be spent on the Galactic front  lines in battlefront two's multiplayer  while the 40 player conquest game type  galactic assault will likely be the most  popular game mode the upgraded  Starfighter assault deserves some  recognition  now with more responsive and tighter  controls for maneuvering your vessels  the aerial focus mode features  battlefront tuesd most intense missions  along with some stellar visual and sound  design the large-scale battles have the  same exciting flow as Star Wars as most  iconic fights where one heroic action  can turn the tide of a conflict another  victory for the first-order  your set of troopers starfighter classes  and hero characters can be boosted up  with star cards by amplifying stats  adding bonus attributes and even giving  characters alternate loadouts such as  replacing a heavy troopers energy shield  for a grenade launcher the number of  ways you can modify your characters is  impressive  [Music]  unfortunately this is where battlefront  two's multiplayer progression begins to  show its cracks getting a handle the  many systems gates and subtle nuances  therein can be confusing and off-putting  after each battle you'll collect  experience for your overall rank and  also credits to use for purchasing loot  crate's in the in-game store by relying  on randomly yielded weapons resources  and star cards of varying grades  battlefront - largely ties its  progression to dice rolls while you can  acquire an upgrade star cards on your  own by using crafting components found  from loot crate's this also leads into  the general problem of gating to upgrade  a card you have to ensure that your  class level and overall multiplayer  ranking meets certain standards while  also spending the fruits of your labor  on loot crate's for more resources and  cards simply focusing on the characters  and classes you like to play isn't  enough and the system in place all too  easily puts walls in front of you due to  the randomness and the inherent  dependence on the loot crate's  progression is often dictated by what  these results are this steers you away  from the classes you like and more  annoyingly results in cards for hero  characters you have yet to unlock this  is all made worse by the cumbersome menu  system which prompts you to exit out of  multiplayer games to collect your  rewards from milestones and challenges  while also obscuring vital information  such as player rank and class data  shortly before battlefront 2's official  launch ea removed its premium currency  due to negative reaction from the early  access period in the form of crystals  this particular resource which we were  able to buy and packs up to $100 was  used to circumvent credits for  purchasing loot boxes while this removal  may seem like a welcome change the rest  of the progression system remains  unaltered leaving you with fewer options  to deal with an already steep ride the  grind in Battlefront 2 will force you to  reconcile with the fact that the time  you invest in the game won't always be  rewarded with progress or at least in  the way you want but above all the  biggest problem with the system is that  it's never clearly explained while  you'll eventually come to understand the  value of credits and other resources  this realization comes as a result of  how little the game offers them in  addition to the arcade boat time gaining  mission rewards upon completion many of  the rewards from battle and milestone  challenges offer scraps for the  sometimes large efforts though the  prices of heroes have been significantly  slashed trying to save up for them is  still a large exercise in patience and  frustration as stockpiling your credits  it means you won't be able to buy the  expensive  Vital Luke rates in this way Battlefront  to plants itself in the same territory  as free to play games with much of its  content and characters tucked away  behind progression walls randomized loot  boxes and gameplay timers above all it  ends up doing a disservice to the core  gameplay itself which can still provide  those solid moments of enjoyment despite  the looming presence of its progression  systems many of these issues related to  the meta game fall by the wayside when  you're in the thick of battle as you're  taking part in the massive struggle  throughout the many locales in the Star  Wars universe while its main narrative  feels unresolved and the general loop of  the multiplayer carries a number of  issues Battlefront 2 still manages to  evoke the same sense of joy and  excitement found in the core of what the  series is all about but as it stands the  biggest hurdle that battlefront 2 will  need to overcome for its simultaneous  attempts to balance complex progression  with the genuine feeling of  accomplishments is deciding on what type  of game it wants to be  [Music]  Oh

